<main><section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">NLP · Research · Public Health</div><h1>NLP Stance Detection</h1><p class="section-lead">A research programme using natural-language processing to classify stance and study public-health discourse across COVID-19 and Mpox.</p></div></section><section><div class="container content"><h2>Problem</h2><p>Real-world public-health text is noisy, ambiguous and context dependent. Useful analysis requires more than sentiment: it needs a defensible definition of stance and a classification process that can be inspected and validated.</p><h2>Approach</h2><ul><li>Define stance labels around the research question.</li><li>Use modern NLP classification methods on messy real-world text.</li><li>Compare behaviour across related health contexts rather than treating each dataset in isolation.</li><li>Keep interpretation tied to what the data and model can actually support.</li></ul><div class="callout">The research emphasis is on extracting useful structure from language without overstating what an automated classifier can infer about people or populations.</div><h2>What this case proves</h2><p>I can take an NLP research question from framing through classification, validation and interpretation, while communicating results in a form suitable for research and practical discussion.</p><p><a href="/case-studies">← Back to case studies</a></p></div></section></main>
